   "source": [
    "# 08 · The Crypt — Model Comparison and Error Analysis\n",
    "\n",
    "[![Open In Colab](https://colab.research.google.com/assets/colab-badge.svg)](https://colab.research.google.com/github/Cecile1015/emotion-mirror-cbs4702/blob/main/notebooks/08_error_analysis.ipynb)\n",
    "\n",
    "Steps 2–6 built a **ladder of text representations**, from counts of words to contextual embeddings, and evaluated each model once on the test split. This notebook brings all models together and asks what is still wrong — and why.\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **The final ledger** (§2): one table for all models in the order of the representation ladder, a significance test for every step up the ladder, and per-class F1.\n",
    "2. **Confusion matrices** (§3) of the best model (fine-tuned BERT) and the TF-IDF + LR baseline.\n",
    "3. **Error analysis** (§4): how the labels of the dataset were assigned, how much of the remaining error is annotation noise, a linguistic coding scheme for errors, and 18 annotated error examples.\n",
    "4. **Disagreement analysis** (§5): the texts on which TF-IDF + LR and BERT disagree, and the words each model relies on.\n",
    "5. **Behavioural tests** (§6): 60 hand-written sentences that probe negation, sarcasm, intensity and mixed emotions (Ribeiro et al., 2020), run through every model including the LLM.\n",
    "6. **Export** (§7) of the results for the web application.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Data use.** No model is trained or selected here. The test split was used once per model in Steps 2–6; this notebook only re-reads the saved predictions (`results/predictions/`) and describes the errors. Everything that is *learned* from data in this notebook — the keyword lexicon of §4 and the context classifiers of §4.2 — is fitted on the **training split only**."
   ]
  },
