  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "2e4e4622",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Interpretation.** Three of the six steps up the ladder are significant after the Holm adjustment: the discriminative classifier (75 texts lost, 238 gained) and the sequence model over averaged vectors (47 vs 561) in the expected direction, and averaged GloVe vectors against sparse features (497 vs 76) in the opposite one. Three steps are **not** significant: TF-IDF weighting (38 vs 35), the Step 3 improvements (71 vs 78) and, most notably, **contextual self-attention on top of the BiLSTM** (55 vs 52, p = 0.85; Δ macro-F1 +0.002 [−0.013, +0.017]). The overall gain from the baseline to the best model is clear: BERT corrects 163 texts that TF-IDF + LR gets wrong and loses 66 (Δ +0.064 [+0.040, +0.087]). The prompted LLM falls significantly below BERT on the same 300 texts (6 vs 63, Δ −0.212).\n",
    "\n",
    "A non-significant step does not show that the two representations are equivalent in general; it shows that this test set cannot separate them. §4 explains why: most of the errors that remain for the two strongest models lie on texts whose label cannot be recovered from the text, so the room for any further representation to improve is very small."
   ]
  },
