  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "c865e79b",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Interpretation.** The figure separates the models by *where* their errors come from rather than by how many they make. The number of split-keyword errors (A1, blue) is almost the same for every model (108–148): no representation can learn a coin flip. What changes along the ladder is everything else. Naive Bayes and GloVe + LR also fail on texts with a reliable keyword and no complication (F: 114 and 259 errors), the sparse logistic regressions still fail when **several emotion words compete** (C: 43–51), and the two sequence models have almost nothing left outside A1: BERT has 19 errors in total in categories A2–F, the BiLSTM 29.\n",
    "\n",
    "The three error types that matter for the best model are, in order of size:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **Annotation noise** (A1 + A2: 122 of 140 errors) — inconsistent labels for the same emotion word, conflicting duplicates and labels that contradict the text.\n",
    "2. **Implicit emotion** (B: 8) — event descriptions without an emotion word, where the emotion must be inferred from world knowledge.\n",
    "3. **Rare words and composition** (E, C, D: 9) — unseen or rare keywords split into misleading WordPieces, competing cues, and negation or modality."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "ea1fbcf5",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### 4.5 Annotated error examples\n",
    "\n",
    "Eighteen misclassified test texts of the best model, chosen to cover every category and every linguistic level. The explanations (`data/handcrafted/error_examples.csv`) were written after reading each text together with the training statistics of its keyword. Two diagnostics are added: the **automatic category** of §4.4, to compare rule and close reading, and **BERT occlusion** — the word whose deletion lowers the probability of BERT's (wrong) prediction the most, a perturbation-based explanation that, unlike attention weights, measures an effect on the output."
   ]
  },
