    "lengths = np.array([len(t.split()) for t in X_test])\n",
    "quartiles = np.percentile([len(t.split()) for t in X_train], [25, 50, 75])\n",
    "length_bin = np.digitize(lengths, quartiles, right=True)\n",
    "length_names = [f\"≤ {int(quartiles[0])} words\", f\"{int(quartiles[0]) + 1}–{int(quartiles[1])} words\",\n",
    "                f\"{int(quartiles[1]) + 1}–{int(quartiles[2])} words\", f\"> {int(quartiles[2])} words\"]\n",
    "train_vocab = training_vocabulary(X_train)\n",
    "slice_order = {\"gold class\": LABELS, \"feel template\": TEMPLATE_LEVELS,\n",
    "               \"negation cue\": [\"without negation cue\", \"with negation cue\"], \"length\": length_names,\n",
    "               \"unseen word\": [\"all words seen\", \">= 1 unseen word\"],\n",
    "               \"split keyword\": [\"without split keyword\", \"with split keyword\"]}\n",
    "slices = {\n",
    "    \"gold class\": gold_test,\n",
    "    \"feel template\": template_slice(X_test),\n",
    "    \"negation cue\": np.where(has_negation(X_test), \"with negation cue\", \"without negation cue\"),\n",
    "    \"length\": np.array([length_names[b] for b in length_bin], dtype=object),\n",
    "    \"unseen word\": np.where(unseen_word_mask(X_test, train_vocab), \">= 1 unseen word\", \"all words seen\"),\n",
    "    \"split keyword\": np.where(has_split, \"with split keyword\", \"without split keyword\"),\n",
    "}\n",
    "slice_rows = []\n",
    "for family, levels in slices.items():\n",
    "    for level in slice_order[family]:\n",
    "        mask = levels == level\n",
    "        n = int(mask.sum())\n",
    "        slice_rows.append({\"slice\": family, \"level\": level, \"texts\": n,\n",
    "                           \"TF-IDF+LR accuracy\": lr_ok[mask].mean(), \"BERT accuracy\": bert_ok[mask].mean(),\n",
    "                           \"BERT only\": np.mean(~lr_ok[mask] & bert_ok[mask]),\n",
    "                           \"LR only\": np.mean(lr_ok[mask] & ~bert_ok[mask])})\n",
    "disagreement = pd.DataFrame(slice_rows)\n",
    "disagreement[\"net gain of BERT\"] = disagreement[\"BERT only\"] - disagreement[\"LR only\"]\n",
    "disagreement.to_csv(METRICS_DIR / \"disagreement_slices_tfidf_bert.csv\", index=False)\n",
    "display(disagreement.style.format({c: \"{:.3f}\" for c in disagreement.columns[3:]}).hide(axis=\"index\")\n",
    "        .bar(subset=[\"net gain of BERT\"], align=\"zero\", color=[\"#eb6834\", \"#2a78d6\"]))\n",
    "\n",
    "fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(9, 8))\n",
    "plot = disagreement.iloc[::-1].reset_index(drop=True)\n",
    "y = np.arange(len(plot))\n",
    "ax.barh(y, plot[\"BERT only\"], color=\"#2a78d6\", edgecolor=\"white\", linewidth=1.5, label=\"only BERT correct\")\n",
    "ax.barh(y, -plot[\"LR only\"], color=\"#eb6834\", edgecolor=\"white\", linewidth=1.5, label=\"only TF-IDF + LR correct\")\n",
    "ax.scatter(plot[\"net gain of BERT\"], y, color=\"black\", s=18, zorder=3, label=\"net gain of BERT\")\n",
    "ax.axvline(0, color=\"#555555\", lw=1)\n",
    "ax.set_yticks(y, [f\"{s}: {l} (n={n})\" for s, l, n in zip(plot[\"slice\"], plot[\"level\"], plot[\"texts\"])], fontsize=8)\n",
    "ax.set_xlabel(\"Share of the texts in the slice\")\n",
    "ax.set_title(\"Disagreements between TF-IDF + LR and BERT, by slice\")\n",
    "ax.legend(loc=\"upper center\", bbox_to_anchor=(0.5, -0.07), ncol=3, fontsize=8, frameon=False)\n",
    "savefig(fig, \"crypt_disagreement_slices.png\")\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "20585a0b",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Interpretation.** The two models agree on 88% of the texts. BERT's net gain is concentrated where a bag of words lacks information: the minority classes *surprise* (+0.318) and *love* (+0.239), the few texts with **no form of feel** (+0.282, n = 39), **long texts** of more than 25 words (+0.117, against +0.016 to +0.029 for shorter texts) and texts with a word never seen in training (+0.078 vs +0.035). A long text contains more words that are not the keyword, so the sum over all word weights of a linear model is diluted; a contextual encoder can attend to the one relevant word wherever it occurs. *Joy* is the only class where BERT loses (−0.019), the counterpart of the joy → love shift of §3.\n",
    "\n",
    "Two results run against a common expectation. BERT gains no more on texts with a **negation cue** than on texts without (+0.049 vs +0.048): self-attention can represent the scope of *not*, but the labels do not reward it (§4.3). And on texts with a **split keyword** both models are right only on different halves (27.2% only BERT, 22.8% only LR, net +0.043): they are flipping different coins."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "b5de068b",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### 5.3 What misleads the linear model?\n",
    "\n",
    "For a softmax regression the decision between the predicted class $k$ and the gold class $g$ depends on the score difference $(\\mathbf{w}_k - \\mathbf{w}_g)^\\top \\mathbf{x} + (b_k - b_g)$, so every word $j$ contributes $x_j\\,(w_{k,j} - w_{g,j})$ (Lecture 5). For each text that only BERT gets right, the word with the largest contribution towards TF-IDF + LR's wrong answer is identified, and compared with the word BERT depends on most for its correct answer (occlusion: the word whose deletion lowers P(gold) the most). Each word is classified as the text's **keyword** (the word after *feel*), **another keyword** of the lexicon (an emotion word in a different position), or **a word outside the lexicon** (function words, *feel* itself, evaluative words such as *good*)."
   ]
  },
