   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "9c85f675",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Interpretation.** 58 of the 66 texts that only TF-IDF + LR gets right (88%) contain a split keyword: BERT's losses are almost entirely coin flips that happen to land differently. Among the few others are the examples of §4.5 where BERT's broader reading hurts: the WordPiece split of *angsty*, the ironic *balmy*, and long texts in which BERT follows a vivid secondary cue (*so glad*) rather than the clause the annotator chose."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "5b06812a",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 6. Behavioural tests\n",
    "\n",
    "Held-out accuracy measures how well a model reproduces the annotation of texts like the training texts. **Behavioural testing** (CheckList; Ribeiro et al., 2020) asks instead whether a model handles specific linguistic capabilities, with small hand-written test sets. The suite in `data/handcrafted/behavioural_tests.csv` has 60 sentences written in the style of the dataset (lower case, no punctuation, contractions as *don t*):\n",
    "\n",
    "| Capability | Sentences | What is tested | Test types |\n",
    "|---|---|---|---|\n",
    "| Negation | 20 | 7 pairs of an emotion statement and its negation (*i feel happy today* / *i don t feel happy today*); 6 sentences whose negation word does **not** reverse the emotion (*i can t stop smiling*) | MFT; DIR: negation must lower P(affirmed emotion) by at least 0.10 |\n",
    "| Sarcasm | 12 | positive words expressing a negative attitude (*i feel so blessed to be stuck at work on christmas eve*); anger or sadness accepted | MFT |\n",
    "| Intensity | 18 | 6 groups: downtoner / no modifier / intensifier (*a little sad* / *sad* / *incredibly sad*) | MFT; INV: same label in the group; DIR: the intensifier must not lower P(emotion) below the downtoner |\n",
    "| Mixed emotions | 10 | two emotions in one sentence (*excited but also terrified*); either label accepted | MFT; both emotions among the two most probable classes |\n",
    "\n",
    "**MFT** (minimum functionality test) checks the label, **INV** (invariance) checks that a label-preserving change leaves the prediction unchanged, **DIR** (directional expectation) checks that a change moves a probability in the expected direction. The expected labels of negated sentences follow the **meaning** of the sentence (*i don t feel happy* → sadness), not the annotation convention of §4.3; §6.4 compares the two."
   ]
  },
