  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "03a583c0",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Interpretation.** Every trained model passes the affirmative controls and the intensity groups (MFT and INV 1.00, except GloVe + LR), and none handles **negation**: the seven negated statements are classified as the affirmed emotion by BoW + LR, both TF-IDF models, the BiLSTM and BERT (0 of 7). **Sarcasm** fails just as clearly (1–4 of 12): *i feel so blessed to be stuck at work on christmas eve* is *love* for BERT. Negation words that do not reverse the emotion are mostly handled (0.67–1.00; 0.83 for BERT), because the keyword carries the label anyway; BERT's one failure, *nothing scares me more than the thought of losing my mother* → sadness, follows the content (losing one's mother) rather than the construction.\n",
    "\n",
    "**The prompted LLM is the exception.** DeepSeek with the few-shot prompt of Step 6 passes **57 of 60** tests (0.95, Wilson interval [0.86, 0.98]), against 41–44 for the trained models, whose intervals all end at 0.83 or below. It reverses 6 of the 7 negated statements, recognises 10 of the 12 sarcastic sentences as anger, and passes every negation-kept, intensity and INV test; all 60 answers were valid JSON. Its three failures are instructive: *i don t feel scared when i walk home alone at night anymore* → fear (the keyword wins), *wow what a wonderful surprise my flight got cancelled again* → surprise (the literal reading of *surprise*), and *i feel so blessed to be stuck at work on christmas eve* → joy. The ranking of §2 is thus **inverted**: the model with the lowest held-out score among the strong models (0.713 on the 300-text subset) has by far the highest behavioural pass rate. The LLM takes no part in the DIR and top-two tests, because its probabilities are degenerate at temperature 0 (Step 6).\n",
    "\n",
    "**Degree modifiers are ignored by the trained models.** The probability of the emotion is not an intensity scale: BERT gives 0.998–0.999 and the BiLSTM 1.000 to *a little sad*, *sad* and *incredibly sad* alike, so the DIR test reduces to rounding noise for them. For the two TF-IDF models the sentence **without** a modifier has the highest probability in all six groups — a downtoner and an intensifier both lower it (TF-IDF + LR on joy: 0.63 / 0.95 / 0.80) — because an extra word dilutes the L2-normalised TF-IDF vector. A classifier trained on categorical labels has no reason to encode intensity.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Mixed emotions** expose overconfidence: either label is accepted, so every model passes the MFT, but BERT ranks the second emotion second in only 3 of 10 sentences, against 7–8 for the other trained models. BERT's probabilities are concentrated on one class even when the sentence names two.\n",
    "\n",
    "Among the trained models the differences in the overall pass rate (0.68–0.73) are not meaningful at this sample size: the ladder that separates them on held-out data (§2) does not separate them on these capabilities. What separates the LLM from them is not the representation alone but the **training signal**: 16,000 texts labelled by a keyword convention on one side, pre-training on a very large corpus followed by instruction tuning on the other."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "ff2aa3e2",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### 6.3 Negation: meaning or convention?\n",
    "\n",
    "For each negated sentence the **convention label** is the training majority label of its emotion keyword (§4.3) — the label the annotation of the dataset would most likely assign. A model that has learned the convention predicts it; a model that understands the negation predicts the expected label."
   ]
  },
