   "source": [
    "negated = suite[suite[\"variant\"] == \"negated\"].copy()\n",
    "negated[\"keyword\"] = [lexicon.keyword(t) for t in negated[\"text\"]]\n",
    "negated[\"convention\"] = [kw_table.at[k, \"majority\"] for k in negated[\"keyword\"]]\n",
    "negated[\"expected\"] = negated[\"expected\"].str.join(\"|\")\n",
    "neg_pred = items[items[\"variant\"] == \"negated\"].pivot(index=\"test_id\", columns=\"model\", values=\"pred_label\")[models_run]\n",
    "neg_view = negated.set_index(\"test_id\")[[\"text\", \"keyword\", \"expected\", \"convention\"]].join(neg_pred.rename(columns=SHORT))\n",
    "display(neg_view)\n",
    "convention_share = pd.DataFrame({\n",
    "    \"= expected (meaning)\": [np.mean([p in e.split(\"|\") for p, e in zip(neg_pred[m], negated[\"expected\"])])\n",
    "                             for m in models_run],\n",
    "    \"= convention (keyword label)\": [(neg_pred[m].to_numpy() == negated[\"convention\"].to_numpy()).mean() for m in models_run],\n",
    "}, index=[SHORT[m] for m in models_run]).T\n",
    "display(convention_share.style.format(\"{:.2f}\"))"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "09a55285",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Interpretation.** For the trained models the answer is unambiguous: on the negated sentences they predict the **convention label** — the label the annotation gives to the keyword — 100% of the time (BoW + LR, both TF-IDF models, BiLSTM, BERT), and the meaning 0% of the time. This is what §4.3 predicts: in training, a negated keyword keeps its label in 94% of the texts, so ignoring negation is the behaviour that the labels reward.\n",
    "\n",
    "The LLM does the opposite: it follows the **meaning** in 6 of 7 negated sentences and the convention in 1. It does so although its prompt states the convention explicitly (*let the named feeling word decide the label*, Step 6) and its 18 demonstrations come from the training split: the knowledge of negation acquired in pre-training overrides the instruction. The test labels **penalise** the same behaviour. In Step 6 (§8.3) the few-shot LLM's accuracy fell from 0.816 on the subset texts without a negation cue to 0.613 on the 93 texts with one, while BERT stayed at 0.952 and 0.925. Part of the LLM's lower test score is therefore the price of reading negation correctly — a disagreement with the annotation, not a failure of understanding. The behavioural tests and the held-out evaluation measure different things, and on this dataset they point in opposite directions."
   ]
  },
